  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on simulated tyre wear and vehicle energy consumption models rather than empirical fleet tracking data.",
      "what": "This study models the total cost of ownership for heavy goods vehicles by comparing conventional and low rolling resistance trailer tyres across long-haul, urban, and mixed-use driving cycles. The analysis integrates a tyre wear model with a vehicle energy consumption model to evaluate how trailer steering systems mitigate tyre scrubbing and affect overall costs.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that low rolling resistance tyres are only cost-effective in urban settings when paired with trailer steering systems, which can save up to £35,000 over a trailer's service life and cut mixed-use operational costs by 4.9%."
    }
